-- title:
--   Fibre regularity ascends along a formally unramified local algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ and $S$ be commutative Noetherian local rings (in the same universe), let $S$ be an $R$-algebra whose structure map $R \to S$ is a local homomorphism, and assume that $S$ is essentially of finite type over $R$ and formally unramified over $R$. Let $\varpi \in R$, and suppose that the quotient $R / (\varpi)$ by the ideal generated by $\varpi$ is a regular local ring whose Krull dimension, as an element of $\mathrm{WithBot}\ \mathbb{N}_\infty$, equals $1$, and that the quotient $S / (\varpi S)$ by the ideal generated by the image of $\varpi$ under the structure map has Krull dimension equal to $1$ as well. The conclusion is that $S / (\varpi S)$ is a regular local ring. Note that the dimension hypothesis on the upper quotient is assumed, not derived, and that regularity of $R/(\varpi)$ is used only through the consequence that its maximal ideal is generated by a single element.
--
--   This is the ascent of regularity of a fibre of relative dimension zero along an unramified map: if the fibre upstairs still has dimension one and the fibre downstairs is a discrete valuation ring, then the fibre upstairs is one too. It is used in the verification that fibres of the modular curve of full level are regular local rings at points where the relevant chart algebras are unramified over the base.
-- source:
